WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.291 --> 00:00:02.874
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:05.520
- What's up, people?

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.000
Michael here to talk about a fellow, bald,

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.270
digital media personality
who, unlike yours truly,

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.350
has inspired the exquisite
wrath of epidemiologists

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.630
and 1970s folk singers alike.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.590
That's right, Mister Joseph James Rogan.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.290
Now, we talked about Rogan's penchant

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.060
for peddling vitamins
with sky-high promises

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.660
in our video about men's wellness,

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:27.840
so be sure to check that out.

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.070
But here's the gist.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.890
Rogan was a stand-up
comedian, turned sitcom star,

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:34.780
turned host of "Fear Factor,"
turned UFC commentator,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:37.470
who made that rare pivot
into global celebrity

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.960
when he started his podcast,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.970
The Joe Rogan Experience in 2009.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.320
It was bought by Spotify in 2020

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.250
for a technically-speaking
(beep) ton of money,

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:50.000
and today it's the most
popular pod in the world.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.070
Rogan's devoted fans regularly
tune in to consume episodes

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.830
that often last longer than
your average REM cycle.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.600
But lately, Rogan's come under fire

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.630
for spreading misinformation about COVID,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.880
interviewing some pretty sus people,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.680
and supplying enough racial slurs

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.790
for multiple compilation videos.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.020
In fact, it's starting to
feel like the only person

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.430
more fervent than a Joe Rogan
fan is a Joe Rogan hater.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.400
So, what makes Joe Rogan such

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.170
a provocative cultural flash point?

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.200
Why does this seemingly
normal specimen of a man

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.340
inspire equally aggressive
amounts of love and anger?

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.420
And ultimately, what
does Rogan's prominence

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.230
say about our society?

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.360
Let's dig in and find out
in this Wisecrack Edition,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.280
What's the Deal with Joe Rogan?

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.540
Now, let's be clear,

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.500
we've posed a question
with no easy answer.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:37.600
Joe Rogan's deal is
innately hard to define.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:40.410
That's in large part because
there really isn't anyone else

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:41.850
doing what he's doing,

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:44.410
at least at his level
of fame and influence.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.490
Sure, he asks people questions

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.510
under the pretext of gathering information

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:50.970
but he doesn't claim to be a journalist.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:53.630
He talks to experts, but
has never himself claimed

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.238
to be an expert on
anything, except maybe porn.

00:01:57.238 --> 00:01:58.071
(Joe blows raspberry)

00:01:58.071 --> 00:01:59.700
- Woo, got it out of my system.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.890
Now I can concentrate
on my life, my career,

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:04.380
and if I meet somebody
I won't be so needy.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:06.020
- [Michael] And he might perform standup,

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.680
but on the show he doesn't
function as a comedian.

00:02:08.680 --> 00:02:11.760
He's unabashedly in search
of genuine discourse

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.050
about a range of topics.
(upbeat music continues)

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:14.300
His listeners often say

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.650
that they see themselves
reflected in Rogan.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.650
He paints himself as an average dude

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.810
who just so happens to
be able to hold his own

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.650
in conversation with everyone

00:02:22.650 --> 00:02:24.980
from comedians to astrophysicists.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.830
- So philosophy and quantum mechanics,

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:28.890
they sort of...

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.800
They share some sort of a border.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.210
- [Michael] Importantly,
Rogan's crafted a platform

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.990
for himself that grants
him complete control

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.250
over his public image.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.310
And he seems to use it

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:42.090
to intentionally baffle
the mainstream media.

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.120
Because Rogan rarely gives interviews,

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:47.390
the only way to understand
him is to actually listen

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:48.850
to his lengthy podcasts.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.940
This gives him a heavily
cultivated outsider status.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.430
That's even as he makes
millions of dollars

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:57.020
off one of the biggest
media companies in the world

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.610
and arguably stands as one of

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.980
the most influential people in America.

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.400
But if Rogan was just
innocuously interviewing

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.710
tons of people he probably wouldn't be

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.640
instigating this level of controversy.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.760
Though, of course, some
of his guest choices

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.082
have been questionable.

00:03:12.082 --> 00:03:16.830
♪ Oh sweet baby Jesus we
are live, Gavin McIness ♪

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.810
- [Michael] But where
Rogan gets slipperier

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:20.990
than a lubed-up butter
sculpture of a dolphin

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.440
is in his actual beliefs.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:25.897
He has said, "If you say
you disagree with me,

00:03:25.897 --> 00:03:27.877
"I probably disagree with me too.

00:03:27.877 --> 00:03:30.120
"I disagree with me all the time."

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.270
And indeed, his worldview

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:34.400
is inherently impossible
to define or pin down.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.260
His opinions are not even
internally consistent.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.940
Any time someone levies a
critique about his views,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.390
as expressed in one episode,

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:43.330
there seems to always be another episode

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:44.810
where he says the opposite.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:46.950
It's a powerful tactic that does not work

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.210
in other forms of media enterprise.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.730
Typically, ideological
consistency is demanded.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.340
It'd be like if the New
York Times Editorial Board

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:57.530
refuted itself every week.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.810
And because any one of
his controversial opinions

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.600
is personally contradicted by him,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:03.900
he can get away with voicing

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.690
just about any viewpoint he wants.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.190
That means he can't
really be held accountable

00:04:09.190 --> 00:04:10.640
the way other public figures,

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:13.320
actually striving for
consistency, might be.

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:16.030
For example, Rogan might mock mask-wearing

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:17.220
with comedian Bill Burr.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:20.810
- I just love how wearing
a mask became this (beep)

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:22.743
soft thing that you were
doing, like being courteous.

00:04:22.743 --> 00:04:24.713
Being courteous, why is it for (beep).

00:04:24.713 --> 00:04:26.650
- It just is so stupid.

00:04:26.650 --> 00:04:28.090
- [Michael] But just a few months earlier,

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:31.130
he hosted epidemiologist
Michael Osterholm,

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.420
who issued grave warnings
about the importance

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.420
of taking the virus seriously.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:37.200
- Well first of all you have to understand

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.040
the timing of it in the sense
that it's just beginning,

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.800
and so in terms of what hurt, pain,

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:46.760
suffering, death, happens so
far is really just beginning.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:49.250
- [Michael] And then a year
later, he gave a platform

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.950
to cardiologist, Peter McCullough,

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.400
who claimed that the pandemic was planned

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.580
by universities like Johns Hopkins.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:59.600
- And it seemed to be completely
organized and intentional

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:02.420
in order to create acceptance for,

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.880
and then promote mass vaccination.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.370
- [Michael] Oh,
consistency, where art thou?

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:10.610
Anyway, by establishing himself
as an open-minded goofball

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.600
who just likes to chat with smart people,

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.520
Rogan creates a unique
intellectual liminal zone

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.370
in which he and his audience
can just ask questions,

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.670
even if those questions
are being posed to and by

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.050
some controversial folks.
(upbeat music continues)

00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:26.770
Across the political spectrum,
Rogan belies comparison.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.910
With conservatives, like
Ben Shapiro or Alex Jones,

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.980
liberals, like the Pod Save America guys,

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.240
or leftists, like streamer Hasan Piker,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.270
the agenda being relayed
is always pretty explicit.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.390
In contrast, Rogan has
cultivated a platform of loyal

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.690
followers who intrinsically
believe in and trust him,

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:46.660
despite not fully knowing
what his beliefs are.

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.130
Perhaps the closest thing we
have today to what Rogan does

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:50.810
is something like "The View,"

00:05:50.810 --> 00:05:52.680
where a group of women sit around and chat

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.720
about a broad range of topics
they lack expertise on,

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.150
but have strong feelings about.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.570
Like Rogan, they'll bring
in experts to learn from,

00:06:00.570 --> 00:06:03.350
to lend credibility, and to
hopefully offer the audience

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:04.760
some intellectual value.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.030
But because there isn't
really a "The View" for men,

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:10.120
many fans see Rogan as providing

00:06:10.120 --> 00:06:13.260
a masculine-coded space that
doesn't exist elsewhere.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.930
Furthermore, Rogan doesn't
talk down to his audience.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:17.160
And that's a big deal,

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.040
because a lot of Rogan
fans see mainstream media

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.470
as being pretty judgy towards
non-mainstream opinions.

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:25.350
But Rogan harbors no judgment

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.130
towards his guests or his audience.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.510
Hell, he's got no judgment for anyone!

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:30.460
Come as you are!

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:33.930
In Rogan's universe, everybody
with an opinion is cool,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.810
from Bernie Sanders to a Proud Boy.

00:06:36.810 --> 00:06:39.990
The only real sin is not
listening to each other.

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:42.390
And that's what makes Rogan so compelling.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:43.950
His listeners don't feel like

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:45.530
they're being told what to think.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.840
They see Rogan fostering open dialogue

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:49.660
across the ideological spectrum,

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:51.570
and then having enough
faith in his audience

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:53.330
to draw their own conclusions.

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.970
Taken at face value, that sounds great.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:58.510
If we buy the curated image
that Rogan has crafted

00:06:58.510 --> 00:07:00.420
of himself and his podcast,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.590
it actually sounds pretty benign.

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:05.710
Now importantly, because of
Rogan's intellectual flexibility

00:07:05.710 --> 00:07:07.600
and willingness to talk to anyone,

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.520
many of his listeners describe him

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.340
as an outside disruptor
speaking truth to power

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.620
and countering mainstream narratives.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.940
This characterization, though,
ignores an important reality,

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.310
these days, Joe Rogan is
as mainstream as it gets.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.880
Indeed, each week his
podcasts get more listeners

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:25.860
than any single evening of programming

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.160
on a major news network.
(upbeat music continues)

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:29.430
Even as he pitches himself as an outsider,

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.240
it's hard to not call him mainstream.

00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:34.630
This apparent contradiction was explained

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:37.100
by the New York Times podcast, The Daily,

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.550
which posits that there are really

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:40.640
two mainstream medias at play here.

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.410
There's what they call the
corporate legacy mainstream,

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:44.810
that is, huge newspapers

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:47.290
like the New York Times or
a television news network.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:49.210
These organizations are supposedly held

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:51.520
to certain journalistic
standards and practices,

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:53.530
like fact checking, source vetting,

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.690
and not spreading misinformation.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:58.060
In practice, this doesn't
always go perfectly,

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.210
but the ideals still stand.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.810
Reporters, editors, legal
teams, and fact checkers

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.000
all collaborate to ensure their content

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.500
is responsible and unimpeachable.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.930
They are machines with
lots of moving parts

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.410
and a lot of money.

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:13.600
This is the establishment mainstream.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:17.520
And then there's Rogan's
mainstream, a sort of Wild West

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.400
where the rules of
journalism no longer apply.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:22.060
While a skilled interviewer,

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:25.100
Rogan's not beholden to those
same standards of research,

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.930
sourcing, fact checking, and so on.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.840
His podcast is informal.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.650
It allows a wide range of viewpoints

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:32.860
without concern for
whether or not those views

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:35.700
are properly backed up by
standards like peer review.

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:38.860
As a result, he's seen as
being anti-establishment,

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:42.050
and allowing those not typically
given a platform to speak.

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.300
So Rogan's not journalism.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.910
But he's also not explicitly
trying to entertain

00:08:46.910 --> 00:08:48.920
in the usual sense of the word.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:50.690
Sure, he'll have funny comedians

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:52.690
and actual conspiracy
theorists on his show

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:55.180
but he'll also invite
high-brow intellectuals.

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:57.480
In a world where the line
between entertainment

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.080
and journalism has become
increasingly blurry,

00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.480
Rogan collapses that dichotomy entirely.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.000
And while his fans may see
themselves as pushing back

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:08.980
against the corporate legacy mainstream,

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.020
they are very much a part
of Rogan's mainstream,

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.770
complete with a huge
voice and a huge platform.

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:17.750
This is no counterculture.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.140
But his mainstream is undoubtedly

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.350
pushing back against gatekeeping.

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:23.980
And the experts and intellectuals

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:25.810
that modern journalism relies on

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:28.620
are often considered
to be doing just that.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:31.750
To understand Rogan's
appeal as the self-defined

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:34.300
common man of the media,
we have to understand

00:09:34.300 --> 00:09:37.440
anti-intellectualism
as a force in America.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.720
Now, there's been a long
standing tension in America

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:43.550
between the so-called elite intelligentsia

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:44.850
and the average Joe.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:47.070
While our earliest
leaders were undoubtedly

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:48.890
the headiest of intellectuals,

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:51.350
men of the mind were met with suspicion

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:55.140
as early as Thomas Jefferson's
presidential run in 1796.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.570
And, in the early 19th century,

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.670
as the ideals of populist democracy

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:00.890
came to the forefront of
the American consciousness,

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:04.380
the goal was to eliminate status
differences in public life.

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.430
According to historian Richard Hofstadter,

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:09.160
the leagues of common
men wanted to get along

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:10.920
with as little leadership as possible

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:13.380
from the educated and propertied class.

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.670
In a country where most people didn't even

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.300
have an elementary education,

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:20.150
where could these common
folks look for guidance,

00:10:20.150 --> 00:10:22.040
if not to learned scholars?
(upbeat music continues)

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.580
Rather than, say, pushing
to expand the realm

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:26.880
of higher education to make it accessible

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.040
to the every man, something else happened.

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.787
As Hofstadter explains,
"As popular democracy

00:10:31.787 --> 00:10:33.527
"gained strength and confidence,

00:10:33.527 --> 00:10:35.827
"it reinforced the widespread belief

00:10:35.827 --> 00:10:39.267
"in the superiority of inborn,
intuitive, folkish wisdom

00:10:39.267 --> 00:10:41.357
"over the cultivated, over sophisticated

00:10:41.357 --> 00:10:42.807
"and self-interested knowledge

00:10:42.807 --> 00:10:45.440
"of the literati and the well-to-do."

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.680
That is to say, the ethos
of 19th century America

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:50.840
was to trust your gut, not a book.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:52.380
We see touches of this mindset

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.040
in some of Rogan's logic-driven,

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:57.390
but ultimately disreputable,
pontificating today.

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.690
For example, Rogan once said
something about COVID vaccines

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.960
that might first strike you
as relatively intuitive.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.960
- But if you're, like, 21 years old

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:07.770
and you say to me,
"Should I get vaccinated?"

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:12.080
I'll go, No. Are you healthy?
Are you a healthy person?

00:11:12.080 --> 00:11:13.950
Like, look, don't do anything stupid

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:15.180
but you should take care of yourself.

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:17.690
If you're a healthy person

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:19.770
and you're exercising all
the time and you're young

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.580
and you're eating well,

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:23.980
I don't think you need
to worry about this.

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.930
- [Michael] Of course, within
the epidemiological community

00:11:26.930 --> 00:11:28.830
there's near-unanimous agreement

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:32.370
that young, healthy people
absolutely should be vaccinated,

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.020
because the issue is more complicated

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:36.270
than Rogan's common sense.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.950
That speaks to an inconvenient
reality about our world.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.780
The Venn diagram of intuition and fact

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:44.660
does not always overlap.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:46.520
Anyway, Rogan addressed the backlash

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:49.820
by essentially abdicating
intellectual responsibility.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:51.370
But back to history.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:54.750
Anti-intellectualism in America
was really off to the races

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:58.360
with the rise of Jacksonian
democracy around 1822.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:01.420
Andrew Jackson's administration
distrusted expertise

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.560
and intellectuals, and
basically kick-started

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:06.650
their expulsion from
political life for decades.

00:12:06.650 --> 00:12:08.947
Now, as author Tom Nichols
points out in his book,

00:12:08.947 --> 00:12:12.250
"The Death of Expertise,"
societies tend to thrive

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.950
when people don't have to
rely on their own expertise

00:12:14.950 --> 00:12:16.680
in every single arena of life.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.680
Back when everybody had to be
able to hunt their own food

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.870
and build their own homes
and make their own clothes

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:26.050
and bind their own wounds,
workmanship was a lot shoddier.

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:28.680
Not having enough time to
devote to knowing one thing

00:12:28.680 --> 00:12:30.240
or a small number of things

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.330
made our ancestors jacks of
all trades and masters of none.

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:35.990
But for a lot of American history,

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:39.530
this kind of self-reliance
by the uneducated common man

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.480
was extolled as virtuous.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.040
At the same time, because
most professions back then

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:46.080
required little or no formal education,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:48.900
intellectualism was seen more as a luxury

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.410
than as a necessity.

00:12:50.410 --> 00:12:53.640
The growing world of business
also emphasized intuition

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:55.810
versus over-intellectualizing.

00:12:55.810 --> 00:12:58.310
Again, not unlike Joe Rogan.
(upbeat music continues)

00:12:58.310 --> 00:13:01.330
But as the world became
increasingly complicated

00:13:01.330 --> 00:13:02.910
via the Industrial Revolution,

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:04.960
it also became increasingly difficult

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:06.840
to be functionally self-reliant.

00:13:06.840 --> 00:13:09.450
Experts with specific
knowledge were brought back

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:12.200
into the fold of political
life in the Progressive Era

00:13:12.200 --> 00:13:14.040
at the end of the 19th century,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.090
where they would basically maintain sway

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:17.830
throughout the New Deal era.

00:13:17.830 --> 00:13:20.490
Back then, their priorities
pretty much aligned

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.520
with those of the aggrieved common man

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.150
who had seen their country hijacked

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:26.420
by the likes of Gilded Age robber barons.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:29.490
But what really changed everything,
according to Hofstadter,

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:32.950
was the rise of anti-Communist
sentiment in America.

00:13:32.950 --> 00:13:35.370
See, a fair amount of public intellectuals

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:38.840
had Communist sympathies and
due to anxieties that climaxed

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:40.770
during the Red Scare of the 1950s,

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:43.650
these experts in everything
from social sciences,

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:45.630
to chemistry, to screenwriting,

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:48.660
were systematically
purged from public life.

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:50.990
Across the board, intellectuals were met

00:13:50.990 --> 00:13:52.840
with a suspicion and hostility that,

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:57.310
as Hofstadter noted in 1963,
was still alive and well.

00:13:57.310 --> 00:13:59.460
But ironically, at the same time

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:02.340
as hostility towards
intellectuals was resurging,

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:05.400
America began needing
experts more than ever.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:08.087
As Hofstadter explained
of early 1960s America:

00:14:08.087 --> 00:14:10.647
"Society has grown greatly in complexity

00:14:10.647 --> 00:14:12.557
"and in involvement with
the rest of the world.

00:14:12.557 --> 00:14:14.007
"In most areas of life,

00:14:14.007 --> 00:14:17.457
"formal training has become
a prerequisite to success.

00:14:17.457 --> 00:14:19.947
"At the same time, the
complexity of modern life

00:14:19.947 --> 00:14:22.037
"has steadily whittled away the functions

00:14:22.037 --> 00:14:24.237
"the ordinary citizen can intelligently

00:14:24.237 --> 00:14:27.250
"and comprehendingly perform for himself."

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:29.570
This only intensified as America

00:14:29.570 --> 00:14:33.190
became increasingly oriented
towards knowledge work.

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:36.200
In the complicated
post-Industrial-Revolution world,

00:14:36.200 --> 00:14:40.290
we can't all know everything,
so we rely on experts.

00:14:40.290 --> 00:14:42.020
An architect to design the building,

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:43.380
an electrician to wire it,

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:45.860
a plumber to make the
toilet work, et cetera.

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:48.350
In situations like this, we understand

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.560
that someone who has been
trained and certified

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:54.500
and worked at it for years is
probably the person you want.

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.030
Even if, like me, you thought
you could fix your toilet

00:14:58.030 --> 00:14:59.730
and then ended up having to number two

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:01.050
at a coffee shop for a week.
(upbeat music continues)

00:15:01.050 --> 00:15:03.630
But, as Nichols notes, this isn't the case

00:15:03.630 --> 00:15:06.440
when it comes to more
heady, less hands-on work.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:09.760
Americans, he writes, tend
to really chafe at the idea

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.730
that we're not all equally as smart

00:15:11.730 --> 00:15:13.580
and capable as the next guy,

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:15.800
especially if we still suspect that guy

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:17.800
might have Communist sympathies.

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:20.780
So even as American resentment
towards intellectuals grew,

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:22.710
we became increasingly reliant on them

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:24.350
in every facet of life,

00:15:24.350 --> 00:15:26.580
thus further augmenting that resentment.

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:28.530
Nichols argues that this hostility

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:30.420
is continued into the present day

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:33.080
and we think that's part of
why Rogan is so appealing.

00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:35.560
He's not an expert and doesn't try to be.

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:37.750
He embodies an overarching ethos that,

00:15:37.750 --> 00:15:40.727
as Hofstadter puts it, "The
plain sense of the common man

00:15:40.727 --> 00:15:43.617
"is an altogether adequate substitute for,

00:15:43.617 --> 00:15:46.127
"if not actually much superior to,

00:15:46.127 --> 00:15:48.720
"formal knowledge and expertise."

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:51.650
And this is an ethos that has
dominated American society

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:53.210
for much of its history.

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:55.330
Now, there's a real psychological reason

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.890
why trusting experts
can feel disempowering,

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:00.360
especially as we've
entered the knowledge era,

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.010
where hugely complex endeavors,

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.570
like sequencing the human genome

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:06.300
or coding self-driving cars
have been accomplished.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:09.050
It's easy to feel like we know nothing

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:11.060
and that's not a fun feeling.

00:16:11.060 --> 00:16:13.117
As Nichols writes, "The fact of the matter

00:16:13.117 --> 00:16:15.007
"is that we cannot
function without admitting

00:16:15.007 --> 00:16:16.387
"the limits of our knowledge

00:16:16.387 --> 00:16:18.927
"and trusting in the expertise of others.

00:16:18.927 --> 00:16:22.297
"We sometimes resist this
conclusion because it undermines

00:16:22.297 --> 00:16:24.467
"our sense of independence and autonomy.

00:16:24.467 --> 00:16:27.167
"This natural human
reaction among individuals

00:16:27.167 --> 00:16:30.097
"is dangerous when it becomes
a shared characteristic

00:16:30.097 --> 00:16:32.080
"among entire societies."

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:35.290
In other words, it's fair to
hate being wrong about stuff.

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:39.060
The problem is when this becomes
a larger societal mindset.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:41.800
Because what happens if
nobody's willing to be wrong,

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:44.610
if every opinion holds equal weight,

00:16:44.610 --> 00:16:47.870
regardless of its grounding
in facts or reality?

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.300
When this becomes a
society's guiding ethos,

00:16:50.300 --> 00:16:52.130
we run the risk of dismantling

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:55.400
the very foundations of
knowledge that enable progress.

00:16:55.400 --> 00:16:57.877
As Nichols notes, today, "The issue is not

00:16:57.877 --> 00:16:59.877
"indifference to established knowledge.

00:16:59.877 --> 00:17:02.367
"It's the emergence of
a positive hostility

00:17:02.367 --> 00:17:03.317
"to such knowledge.

00:17:03.317 --> 00:17:05.867
"It is a new Declaration of Independence.

00:17:05.867 --> 00:17:08.777
"No longer do we hold these
truths to be self-evident,

00:17:08.777 --> 00:17:11.407
"we hold all truths to be self-evident,

00:17:11.407 --> 00:17:13.850
"even the ones that aren't true."

00:17:13.850 --> 00:17:16.710
And that's where we run
into trouble with Joe Rogan.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:19.950
Sure, the guy consults
plenty of reputable experts.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:22.550
But he also consults people
who have been ostracized

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:26.100
by the scientific community
for spreading misinformation

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:27.240
and extolling science

00:17:27.240 --> 00:17:29.870
that hasn't been replicated
or peer reviewed.

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:32.480
He also regularly asks people
their opinion on topics

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:33.770
on which they are not experts
(upbeat music continues)

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:35.910
and probably haven't formed opinions

00:17:35.910 --> 00:17:38.960
with the same rigor as someone
with, say, a graduate degree.

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:41.220
So what happens when
this potpourri of voices

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.000
is heard on the same platform?

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:44.910
The message becomes implicit.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:47.570
All of these truths are equally valid.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:50.290
And when you have some folks
urging people to get vaccines

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:52.190
and some urging you not to,

00:17:52.190 --> 00:17:56.060
the collapse of reliable
truths becomes truly dangerous.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:59.360
Sure, you might choose to take
the expert-certified opinion

00:17:59.360 --> 00:18:01.650
that vaccines save lives to heart,

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:04.700
or you might vibe more
with Rogan's implicit logic

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:07.950
that vaccines are an unnecessary
risk for young people.

00:18:07.950 --> 00:18:11.880
Or you may favor the doctor
whose unprovable anti-vax views

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.540
leave him standing alone
in the medical community.

00:18:14.540 --> 00:18:17.020
But when all opinions
are given equal weight,

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:20.030
choosing who to believe
becomes a trickier prospect.

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:22.830
And this is actually made
all the more complicated

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:24.680
by Rogan's personal affect.

00:18:24.680 --> 00:18:28.090
He has an almost-childlike
sense of credulity,

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:30.440
often acting as impressed, surprised,

00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:32.430
or delighted by someone's opinion.

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:34.830
And sure, Rogan might push back on some of

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:36.430
his most inflammatory guests,

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:38.480
though he does give them plenty of time

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:39.670
to share their opinion.

00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:42.830
But overall, he's more interested
in hearing his guests out

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:44.700
than in engaging in rigorous debate,

00:18:44.700 --> 00:18:47.060
and this is the root of the problem.

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:49.780
To explain, we need to
look to epistemology,

00:18:49.780 --> 00:18:52.670
a branch of philosophy
concerned with investigating

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:54.430
knowledge and human understanding,

00:18:54.430 --> 00:18:56.640
or how we know what we know,

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:59.120
and specifically, the
subsection of that branch

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:01.180
called virtue epistemology.

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:03.760
Virtue epistemologists spend a lot of time

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:06.000
thinking about epistemological virtues,

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:07.900
ie, intellectual virtues

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:10.930
or qualities in a person's
character or disposition

00:19:10.930 --> 00:19:13.520
that contribute to their
ability to acquire knowledge.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:15.960
These include traits
like curiosity, humility,

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.110
objectivity, and creativity.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:21.620
Epistemological vices, as
scholar Eric Kramer explains,

00:19:21.620 --> 00:19:22.530
are the opposite.

00:19:22.530 --> 00:19:24.410
Character traits or thought patterns

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:27.620
that impede or prevent the
acquisition of knowledge.

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:30.530
These include dogmatism,
intellectual dishonesty,

00:19:30.530 --> 00:19:31.670
and wishful thinking.

00:19:31.670 --> 00:19:35.460
The most important epistemological
vice, for our purposes

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:37.980
is gullibility, or credulity.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:41.160
Though the term epistemological
vice is somewhat new,

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:42.880
the concept goes way back.

00:19:42.880 --> 00:19:47.280
In 1661, philosopher Joseph
Glanvill criticized skepticism

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:50.110
and credulity as equal and opposite vices

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:51.800
toward the obtaining of knowledge.

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.340
Of credulity, he described
the human tendency

00:19:54.340 --> 00:19:57.780
to make precipitate,
i.e., hasty judgements

00:19:57.780 --> 00:19:58.960
when we choose to receive
(upbeat music continues)

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:01.710
all things via promiscuous admission.

00:20:01.710 --> 00:20:03.530
Which, coincidentally,
was written about me

00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.970
on a bathroom stall in college.

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:08.390
Basically, while the skeptic
might choose not to accept

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:11.250
even the most persuasive,
well-formed argument,

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:13.130
the credulous person does the opposite.

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:16.030
Credulity implies a lack of discernment,

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:18.750
and credulous folks
tend to accept knowledge

00:20:18.750 --> 00:20:20.680
from the latest person they speak to,

00:20:20.680 --> 00:20:23.170
in spite of qualification
or persuasiveness.

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:25.690
It's being promiscuous with your brain,

00:20:25.690 --> 00:20:27.340
which, to be clear, is different

00:20:27.340 --> 00:20:28.900
than having an emotional affair.

00:20:28.900 --> 00:20:31.440
Credulity, like other
epistemological vices,

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:34.700
thus impedes the careful
acquisition of knowledge.

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:38.160
And that's what we think Joe
Rogan's show is probably doing.

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:40.130
By presenting a broad range of voices

00:20:40.130 --> 00:20:41.810
of varying degrees of credibility,

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:44.750
without rigorous pushback
or a journalistic reliance

00:20:44.750 --> 00:20:47.300
on fact, the show establishes those views

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:49.250
as essentially equally valid.

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:52.503
And we'd argue that Rogan's
much-lauded open-mindedness

00:20:52.503 --> 00:20:55.210
falls more in the camp of credulity.

00:20:55.210 --> 00:20:57.780
What's more, the way
he exhibits this trait

00:20:57.780 --> 00:21:00.440
with nearly every guest
encourages the audience

00:21:00.440 --> 00:21:02.370
to similarly lack discernment.

00:21:02.370 --> 00:21:04.960
In a way, Joe Rogan's success in America

00:21:04.960 --> 00:21:06.660
makes a ton of sense.

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:08.630
He's kind of the contemporary embodiment

00:21:08.630 --> 00:21:10.880
of that old American populist tendency

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:14.470
to trust your gut when you hear
something that sounds right

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:16.610
rather than listening
to the annoying expert

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:18.800
who's been studying the topic for years.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.280
These days, it's become
something of a cliche

00:21:21.280 --> 00:21:24.100
to say that podcasting
comedians like Rogan

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:26.040
are the new philosophers.

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:29.530
But in Rogan's case, epistemic
credulity is precisely

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.280
what makes him not at
all like, say, Socrates,

00:21:32.280 --> 00:21:34.210
the first live-action podcaster.

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:36.260
Socrates' built his philosophical wisdom

00:21:36.260 --> 00:21:38.610
on self-examination and the acceptance

00:21:38.610 --> 00:21:40.950
of one's own ignorance, which meant that

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:43.550
when he had guests,
i.e., Athenian leaders,

00:21:43.550 --> 00:21:45.670
on his podcast, i.e., a street corner,

00:21:45.670 --> 00:21:47.980
he aimed to push them
through a series of questions

00:21:47.980 --> 00:21:50.280
to evaluate the truth of their arguments.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:53.130
Unlike Socrates, Rogan
seems mostly comfortable

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:55.010
with letting everyone
share their opinions,

00:21:55.010 --> 00:21:57.680
with some exceptions, rather
than pushing his guests

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:01.040
and himself towards a deeper
level of self-examination.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:04.520
So, if you're seeking
philosophical wisdom,

00:22:04.520 --> 00:22:06.150
probably stick to YouTube.

00:22:06.150 --> 00:22:07.470
But what do you guys think?

00:22:07.470 --> 00:22:11.270
Is Joe Rogan's show a beacon
of inquiry and wisdom?

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:14.400
Or is it a bastion of
stoner anti-intellectualism?

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.120
Let us know in the comments.

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:17.480
Big thanks to our patrons

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:20.600
and be sure to check out Patreon
page if you have not before

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:24.410
'cause we are about to unveil
some very cool new features.

00:22:24.410 --> 00:22:25.780
Pump up that subscribe button

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:28.070
like you're Rogan's beef-fueled biceps

00:22:28.070 --> 00:22:29.680
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:22:29.680 --> 00:22:31.955
And as always, thanks for watching. Later.

00:22:31.955 --> 00:22:35.372
(upbeat music continues)

